So when we talk about names
like AmoebaNet, Inception,

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:37.920
Transformer, these are
actually ready-to-use models

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:40.194
that you can apply today
to your business problems,

00:10:40.194 --> 00:10:41.610
in the image space,
whether you're

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.550
looking at product
quality on a factory line

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:47.100
or stock outs on a
physical floor space.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.590
So I just want to kind of
clarify that these actually

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:50.340
are ready to use.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.480
They're not just machine
learning techniques.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.660
These are fully
architected models that

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:57.906
are ready to use on your data.

00:10:57.906 --> 00:10:59.780
And then moving forward,
we want to highlight

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.570
one customer that was an
early user of TPU pods, eBay.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.870
WES WAHLIN: Yeah,
so eBay came to us.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:08.360
And they wanted to be able
to speed up their training.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:12.980
Currently they had done a
massive amount of On-Prem work.

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:15.800
And it took them quite
a while to do training,

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.510
and sometimes even took months.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:23.240
We were able to provide
them with some new model

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:27.470
and new model support,
as well as some TPUs,

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:31.370
and were able to increase
their training by over 100x,

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:34.970
as well as increasing their
accuracy 10%, which accuracy

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.602
is kind of the name of the
game, how high can you get it.

00:11:37.602 --> 00:11:39.560
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Yeah,
I mean the performance

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.256
results are really amazing.

00:11:41.256 --> 00:11:43.130
But really let's talk
about the size of this.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:46.830
Why were TPU pods
really useful here?

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:50.080
And if you look at just the
catalog listing of eBay,

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:52.880
one of the staples of Silicon
Valley, one billion product

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:54.350
listings at this point as well.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.150
And for the test that
we did with them,

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:59.450
they actually ran a model on
55 million product images,

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:02.310
and to be able to predict over
10,000 product categories.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:04.520
So this is a very, very
large scale problem

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.190
that would not be able to be
solved without Custom Hardware

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:07.832
Accelerators.

00:12:07.832 --> 00:12:09.290
WES WAHLIN: Yeah,
and the largeness

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:12.870
of how much memory is available
on the TPU pods is key.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.120
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Absolutely.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.880
So now let's jump into how
do you actually get started.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:19.320
Before we dive into that,
I want to talk about

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:20.900
who is this for really?

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:22.760
How are we positioning TPU pods?

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:25.070
The easy answer might be
this is for the deep learning

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.050
researchers and the
data scientists.

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:28.840
And that answer is correct.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:31.340
It's going to allow you to
scale and amplify your work.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.500
But really the broader
mission of Google Cloud

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:36.050
is also to enable the
developers, the data analysts,

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:38.150
the business analysts,
the 20 plus million

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:40.910
out there that want to
leverage machine learning.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.910
So TPUs today, if you
know Python, especially

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:45.920
with these reference
models that Wes mentioned,

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:49.430
you can get started today on
practical pragmatic business

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:50.720
applications.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.120
Also, we understand that
you might have already

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:56.120
done a lot of work on
developing a ML infrastructure.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.010
We really want to make sure
we have minimum disruption.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.770
So if you do want
to leverage TPU pods

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:03.920
for training large models,
you can still take that model

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:07.670
and still serve them within
your existing CPU or GPU serving

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:09.990
infrastructure.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:12.890
Now how do we actually
provision a TPU pod?

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.420
Everything is
through Google Cloud.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:16.820
You log into the Google
Cloud Platform console.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.920
And really actually
it's just one command.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:21.840
There's a nice utility
tool where in this case,

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.270
it's CTPU up.

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:26.510
And then you specify the size
that Wes mentioned, anything

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:29.420
from the normal TPU
device with eight cores,

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:32.060
all the way up to
a full pod of 512.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:35.900
But what exactly does
this CTPU up command do?

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:37.890
And it does a number of things.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:42.140
Number one, it provisions
a host VM, a CPU.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:44.630
This is pre-installed
with TensorFlow.

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:47.060
Today it's with
TensorFlow version 1.12.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.490
But you can specify the
version of your needs.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:55.260
And what the CPU actually
acts as the TensorFlow client.

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:56.780
This has a TensorFlow code.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:59.000
It actually serves up
the TensorFlow graph

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:00.380
to the TPU pod.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:02.780
It also takes all
the images that

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.970
are stored in Google
Cloud Storage bucket

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:06.890
and feeds it into the TPU pod.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:10.910
And the TPU pod essentially
acts as the TensorFlow server

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:13.390
in this case.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.400
Also while you're
using this, it's

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.520
important to note that TPU pods
are accessible through two APIs

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:21.610
currently, the TPU
Estimator and Keras,

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:24.400
for those who are more
familiar with that framework.

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:26.470
There's a number of
tools that are expanding

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:30.310
around being able to monitor
and profile your TPU workloads.

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.350
TPU profiler allows
you to take a look

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:34.120
at a lot of metrics
like utilization

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:37.180
to make sure that TPUs are
being used most effectively.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:39.640
And TPU compatibility
checker is a plugin

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:42.820
to TensorBoard with a screenshot
in the right side of the slide

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.160
to make sure that
your actual TensorFlow

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:46.929
code is optimized for TPUs.

00:14:46.929 --> 00:14:48.970
There's a great YouTube
video in the bottom right

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.336
from our colleague Brennan
during the TensorFlow Def

00:14:51.336 --> 00:14:52.960
Summit that talks a
bit more around all

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:57.780
the tools available to you
as you develop on TPU pods.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:01.500
So now we do want to walk
you through a practical demo

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.720
and first going through the
end to end demo architecture.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:06.510
So first, we start
with the raw images.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:09.780
And those are hosted on our
object storage web service

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:11.490
called Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.530
In this case, I'll go through
an example around ImageNet.

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:16.900
Second, we really want
to pre-process data,

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:19.860
whether it be feature
engineering on tabular

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:22.380
data, data augmentation
for images,

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:26.010
we want a quick way to get
your data ready for training,

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.290
and in this case, also
converting to TF records.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:31.230
And that's really where Apache
Beam or a managed service

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:33.240
on Cloud Dataflow comes in.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.810
Finally, once the data is
ready in Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.140
you submit that to the TPU
pod using the architecture

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:43.630
with the host VM, and the
TPU that I mentioned before.

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:46.470
And finally, the model is
outputted into a storage bucket

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:49.980
for you to use as needed,
or even export and use it

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:51.760
as part of your
current infrastructure.

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:53.968
Finally, there's a lot of
different options to serve.

00:15:53.968 --> 00:15:55.567
One of them is to
serve those models

00:15:55.567 --> 00:15:57.900
on our serving infrastructure,
on Cloud Machine Learning

00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:58.570
Engine.

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:00.194
So this is a great
end to end pipeline.

00:16:00.194 --> 00:16:02.340
And we also suggest
that you have a method

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.130
to orchestrate and automate
this using Cloud Composer, which

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:08.100
is our managed Apache
Airflow solution.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.290
If you were you were watching
the webinar before us,

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:13.320
with Anand and Till, around
Kubeflow pipeline's AI hub,

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.300
really see a reference
architecture like this,

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:18.090
a great asset to reuse
across the organization.

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:20.010
So we really are going
to put in a lot of work

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:22.590
to take this pipeline
with TPU pods

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:25.080
and really add it into
a Kubeflow pipeline

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:28.080
and make it more discoverable
as part of the broader AI hub

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:28.980
community.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:32.460
So now let's jump
into the actual demo.

