00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:53.310
WES WAHLIN: Yeah.

00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:54.768
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA:
Second question,

00:26:54.768 --> 00:26:56.460
can you run custom
models on TPU pods?

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:59.126
We've gone through a lot of this
session talking about reference

00:26:59.126 --> 00:27:02.670
models, ready-to-use models
in the spaces of image,

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:04.340
language, and video.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:07.170
But yes, you can run
custom models on TPU pods.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:09.750
There are certain libraries
within the TensorFlow

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:12.720
framework, TPU Rewrite
and TPU Estimator

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:15.600
that allow you to port over
your existing TensorFlow

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:19.020
models into models that run
efficiently on TPU pods.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:21.812
They just take a little
bit of extra work.

00:27:21.812 --> 00:27:22.520
WES WAHLIN: Cool.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:23.410
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Wes, you
want to take the next question?

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:24.826
WES WAHLIN: Yeah,
so next question

00:27:24.826 --> 00:27:26.460
is, what are petaflops?

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:29.460
So when we think about
like really large compute,

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:33.390
we start talking about how
big and how fast they go.

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:35.640
So when you talk
about a flop, that's

00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:38.482
a floating point
operation per second.

00:27:38.482 --> 00:27:39.940
And when you talk
about a petaflop,

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:41.810
you're talking about
1,000 teraflops.

00:27:41.810 --> 00:27:45.090
So that when you go and
you do this compute,

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:47.080
you can do it that much faster.

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:50.450
So it's just large, large
scale supercomputing.

00:27:50.450 --> 00:27:52.980
And the next step up
from that is exaflop.

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:57.780
So right now with TPU pods,
we're at 11.5 petaflops.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:27:59.370
So that's the
amount of throughput

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:02.850
that you're able to get
with your matrix math.

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:04.740
And let's see, and
then the next one.

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:08.070
Starting at the smallest
TPU size, what changes do

00:28:08.070 --> 00:28:10.915
I need to make to
scale to a TPU pod.

00:28:10.915 --> 00:28:12.540
Well, that we've made
that very simple.

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:14.340
And on that one slide
we talked about how

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:16.590
there's no code changes.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:18.970
The basic change that you
have to make is batch size.

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:21.640
And that's because the
memory is increasing.

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:23.580
So you want to increase
the batch size.

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:27.000
And that's how you keep
the TPU pod fully loaded.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:29.190
So every time that
systolic array

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:31.560
goes and starts
chunking through all

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:33.870
of your layers
and your DNN, it's

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:36.280
going to always be full of data.

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:39.330
And so that's how you
get all the way up

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:41.206
to a full pod, so a
change in the batch size.

00:28:41.206 --> 00:28:42.871
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA:
That's a great point.

00:28:42.871 --> 00:28:45.420
Code changes for the actual
training code, minimal to none,

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:47.086
but you might want
to look into changing

00:28:47.086 --> 00:28:51.160
hyper parameters to fully
utilize the larger slices.

